// Wording for each audience. Unmarked text is the default audience's voice (here the manager).
// `@level:` replaces one-line wording, `@level >` replaces the description, and
// `@level >>` adds a paragraph after it. Every audience sees the same elements.

model payroll: Payroll
  > How staff get paid each month.
  @exec: Payroll at a glance

audiences exec "Executive", manager "Manager" (default), engineer "Engineer" (code)

clerk: Payroll clerk | Runs the monthly pay run [person]

payroll: Payroll system | Works out and pays every salary [system]
  > Takes hours and rates, works out pay and tax, and sends payments to the bank.
  @exec: | Pays our people correctly and on time
  @exec > Keeps pay right and on time, so staff trust it and the company stays within the law.
  @engineer: | payroll-svc, Django 5 :8000
  @engineer >> One Django project, `payroll/`, deployed as a single container. Pay runs are
    Celery tasks on the `payruns` queue.

bank: Bank | Moves the money [external]
  @engineer: | Bank payments API over HTTPS

clerk starts the monthly pay run in payroll
payroll sends payments to bank
  > One payment per person, sent as one batch.
  @engineer: POSTs a payment batch to
  @engineer >> `payroll/bank/client.py` signs the batch with the key in `BANK_SIGNING_KEY`.

view big-picture: Big picture
  show clerk, payroll, bank
  @exec: Who is involved
